WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.159
Over the past 50 years, boys

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.959
and men have lost ground at school

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.029
and work, and they're living shorter

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.109
lives.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.399
They're less likely than women to

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.629
graduate from high school and

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.479
college or to earn advanced degrees.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.949
They're dropping out of the labor

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.959
force in record numbers and account

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.179
for two thirds of so-called deaths

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.579
of despair stemming from suicide,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.069
alcoholism and drug overdoses.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.599
The Brookings Institution scholar

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.494
Richard V.

00:00:25.495 --> 00:00:27.329
Reeves documents these and other

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.189
equally dark developments in

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.499
his new book of Boys and Men

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.149
Why the Modern Male is Struggling,

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.279
Why It Matters and What To Do About

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.109
It. He analyzes the structural

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.029
factors exacerbating these trends,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.799
such as the changing nature of work

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.929
in a post-industrial economy

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.759
and suggests solutions that don't

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.519
come at the expense of women.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.109
Reeves was our guest at the Reason

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.509
Speakeasy, a monthly unscripted

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.459
conversation in New York City with

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.619
outspoken defenders of free thinking

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.599
and heterodoxy in an era

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.280
of conformity and groupthink.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.949
This is the Reason interview with

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:07.809
Nick Gillespie.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.807
Today's guest is Richard V.

00:01:09.808 --> 00:01:11.659
Reeves. He's a scholar

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.699
at the Brookings Institution

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.469
down in Washington, D.C.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:16.849
and he's originally from England.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.859
We're going to ask him about that.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:19.649
I don't know.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.349
It seems problematic, but his latest

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.239
book and he's written many

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.339
is of Boys and Men Why the Modern

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.289
Male is Struggling, Why It Matters

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:28.309
and What to do about it.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.589
So please give a big hand to Richard

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:31.590
V. Reeves.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.499
Richard, thanks for talking.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.539
Let's start with what is

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.909
the elevator pitch

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:43.910
of this book.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.749
That in many domains of life,

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.639
boys and men are now the ones

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.319
who are a disadvantage, including

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.329
some some aspects of education,

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.189
some aspects of employment and

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:54.999
in family life.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.189
And that's especially true for

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.079
working class men and black boys

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.549
and men. And we've got to a point

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.379
now. Such has been the incredible

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:03.909
progress that we've made on gender

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.289
equality, on behalf of women and

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.119
girls, although there's still

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.829
further to go in many areas, that is

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:10.659
now not only possible to look

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:11.919
at gender inequalities that are

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.269
running the other way, but in my

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.159
view, necessary, and that failing

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.569
to do so will actually create all

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.889
kinds of political and cultural

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:18.699
problems.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.709
So let's one of the things

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.539
that is amazing about the book

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.129
is the way that you document this.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.019
Let's let's we're going to spend

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.969
some time going over the kind

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.039
of the extent of the issue or what

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.899
are the indicators that men

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.519
are not doing well.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.529
And some of this is because they

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.209
are, you know, relative to women,

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:38.499
they're falling behind others.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.869
It's just where they were 20, 50,

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:41.689
70 years ago.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:42.969
They're falling behind.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.619
So let's work through some of that.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.449
What I

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.509
guess a good starting place is

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.429
when Title IX was passed in

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.109
the early seventies, which

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.209
conditioned federal funding

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.099
in educational institutions

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.469
that get federal dollars, they need

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.399
to have equal opportunities

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.319
for men and women.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.269
The disparity of women

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.219
in college to men

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.049
was smaller than it is

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.699
now. Yes. Is that right?

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.319
Let's start with that and then run

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.179
through your list of leading

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.159
indicators of where men are not

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:15.819
doing so well.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:16.179
Yeah.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.709
So I really like how you made that

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:18.699
distinction, Nick, right at the

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.529
beginning between a relative

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.749
issue where there's an inequality

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.459
and an absolute issue where we're

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.829
actually seeing an absolute decline.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:28.029
So if you look at education, for

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:28.899
example, you're quite right.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.759
In 1972, when Title IX

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.749
was passed.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.579
Men were 13 percentage

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.379
points more likely to get a college

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.899
degree, a four year college degree.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.669
Title IX was passed within about 15

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.039
years the gap had closed, and

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.329
the most recent numbers show that

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:45.339
women are now 15 percentage points

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.539
more likely to get a four year

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.279
college degree than men are.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.319
So the gender inequality

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.489
in higher education is actually

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.319
slightly wider today than it was in

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.189
1972.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:55.899
It's just it's just flipped.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.069
It's reversed and the other way.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.049
And it's when you look

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.999
at the number of men going to

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.589
college versus women.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:04.449
Bigger gap or what's what's going

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:05.019
on. Yeah.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.129
And it's actually even bigger.

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:07.779
It's a bit bigger there. So college

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.759
campuses now, it's about 60, 60%

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.709
women, 40% men,

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.779
a little bit less true in private

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.669
colleges because private colleges

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:16.869
are allowed to discriminate on the

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:17.949
basis of sex.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:19.208
So they're now discriminating in

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:20.889
favor of men to try and hold

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:22.569
themselves closer to 5050.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.819
But across the board, it's now 6040.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.229
And so even when men do go to

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.849
college, they're less likely to

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:28.929
complete college.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.309
So there's both an enrollment gap.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:31.539
It's roughly ten percentage point

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.519
enrollment gap enrolling

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.199
and then roughly a similar gap in on

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.609
time completion.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.139
So men are less likely to go, but

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:39.099
they're also less likely to

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.569
complete. The result of that is that

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:41.349
15 point gap.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.239
And at, you

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.219
know, in graduate school, whether

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.049
it's law degrees or medical degrees,

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:48.519
we're also seeing something similar,

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:48.999
right?

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:49.779
Yeah.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.489
Much more recently, women now

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.479
account for the majority of

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.009
postgraduate degrees, including

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.329
PhDs.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.459
That is a that's more recent masters

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:58.419
degrees, actually.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.999
That was probably ten years ago,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.009
BAs probably 20 years ago.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.539
And so basically what we're seeing

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.009
is there's kind of this ripple

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:06.849
effect all the way through the

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:07.989
education system now.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.669
So there there is no level of

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.289
education now where the gender gap

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.089
doesn't doesn't go in favor of women

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:15.009
and girls, whereas, of course, you

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.359
don't have to go back all that far

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:17.529
to discovered to find that it was

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:18.610
completely the other way around.

